+### [Stochastic Depth Rendering](23216-stochastic-depth-rendering.md)
+Stochastic Depth Rendering is a method for computing depth maps from 3D Gaussian primitives by modeling them as continuous stochastic solids. It solves the problem of noisy, inconsistent depth estimates in standard Gaussian Splatting by defining depth as the median transmittance crossing ($T=0.5$) rather than an opacity-weighted average. Unlike alpha-averaged expected depth, which blurs silhouettes and suffers from view-dependent floater bias, this approach provides sharper boundary transitions and superior multi-view consistency. It builds on [Continuous Stochastic Solids Volume Rendering](18428-continuous-stochastic-solids-volume-rendering.md) by applying the stochastic solid attenuation model specifically to depth estimation.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** The method requires sorting primitives and integrating transmittance along rays, which is comparable to standard rasterization but involves additional bisection iterations for depth solving [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing Gaussian parameters and intermediate transmittance values along the ray [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves geometric accuracy and depth synthesis quality by avoiding floating-depth artifacts. Provides smoother depth maps compared to rasterization-based median depth [S1, unofficial]. Decouples normal supervision from depth rendering, allowing direct normal rendering from primitives [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves geometric accuracy and depth synthesis quality by avoiding floating-depth artifacts [S1, unofficial]. Provides smoother depth maps compared to rasterization-based median depth [S1, unofficial]. Decouples normal supervision from depth rendering, allowing direct normal rendering from primitives [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** More complex than standard rasterization due to the volume rendering formulation and bisection iterations [S1, unofficial].
 *   **When to Use:** When accurate scene geometry and depth maps are critical, such as in visual geometry grounded transformers or generalizable foundation models [S1, unofficial].
